"""
scripts/webfundamentals_part4.py
Fourth batch of fresher Web Fundamentals interview questions.
"""

web_part4_items = [
    ('What is the BroadcastChannel API in modern browsers?', 'The BroadcastChannel API allows simple bidirectional communication between different browser tabs, windows, or iframes sharing the exact same origin.', 'Easy', 'Practical', "const channel = new BroadcastChannel('app_channel');\nchannel.postMessage('User logged out');\nchannel.onmessage = (e) => {\n  if (e.data === 'User logged out') logoutUser();\n};", 'How is this useful for synchronizing user logout across all open tabs?'),
    ('How do you detect when the user loses or regains internet connectivity in the browser?', 'Listen to the `online` and `offline` window events, or check `navigator.onLine` boolean property.', 'Easy', 'Practical', "window.addEventListener('online', () => showBanner('Back online!'));\nwindow.addEventListener('offline', () => showBanner('You are offline'));", 'Can `navigator.onLine` report true even if the router has no internet connection?'),
    ('What is the PerformanceObserver API?', 'An API used to collect and observe performance metric entries (like LCP, CLS, longtasks, resource timings) asynchronously as they occur on the page.', 'Intermediate', 'Practical', "const observer = new PerformanceObserver((list) => {\n  list.getEntries().forEach((entry) => {\n    console.log(entry.name, entry.startTime);\n  });\n});\nobserver.observe({ type: 'largest-contentful-paint', buffered: true });", 'What does `buffered: true` do?'),
    ("What is a 'Long Task' in browser performance monitoring?", 'Any continuous execution of JavaScript on the main thread that takes longer than 50 milliseconds, which can cause frame drops and noticeable input lag for users.', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'How does yielding to the main thread with `scheduler.yield()` break up long tasks?'),
    ("What does the browser Network Tab waterfall metric 'Waiting for server response (TTFB)' represent?", 'The time spent waiting for the backend server to process the request, query databases, execute business logic, and return the first byte of response data.', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'If TTFB is 3 seconds, is the issue on the client-side or backend server?'),
    ("What does the Network Tab waterfall metric 'Stalled / Blocking' mean?", 'The request was delayed before being sent, commonly because the browser reached its limit of 6 simultaneous TCP connections per domain in HTTP/1.1 or was waiting for disk cache allocation.', 'Intermediate', 'Concept', '', 'How does HTTP/2 eliminate connection stalling?'),
    ("What does the Network Tab waterfall metric 'Content Download' indicate?", 'The time taken to receive all remaining bytes of the response body after the first byte arrived, influenced directly by bandwidth and payload size.', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'How do you reduce Content Download time (compression, minification, tree-shaking)?'),
    ('What is the difference between an absolute URL and a relative URL?', 'An absolute URL includes the full protocol, host, and path (`https://example.com/images/logo.png`). A relative URL specifies a path relative to the current URL (`./logo.png` or `/images/logo.png`).', 'Easy', 'Comparison', '', 'What does a root-relative URL starting with `/` resolve to?'),
    ('What does a protocol-relative URL starting with `//` (e.g. `//cdn.example.com/lib.js`) do?', 'It uses whatever protocol (HTTP or HTTPS) the current host page was loaded with. In modern HTTPS-everywhere web development, explicit `https://` is preferred.', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'Why is explicit `https://` preferred over `//` today?'),
    ('What is the difference between URI encoding and HTML entity encoding?', 'URI encoding (`%20`, `%26`) escapes characters for valid inclusion in web URLs. HTML entity encoding (`&lt;`, `&gt;`, `&amp;`) encodes characters for safe display in HTML markup without being interpreted as tags.', 'Easy', 'Comparison', '', 'Which encoding is used to prevent XSS in HTML text?'),
    ('Which HTTP header specifies the MIME type of the resource being sent in the response?', '`Content-Type` (e.g. `Content-Type: application/json; charset=utf-8`).', 'Easy', 'MCQ', '', {'A': 'Accept', 'B': 'Content-Type', 'C': 'Content-Encoding', 'D': 'Media-Type'}, 'B', 'What is the MIME type for standard JavaScript files?'),
    ('Which HTTP request header specifies the media types the client is able to understand?', '`Accept` (e.g. `Accept: application/json, text/html`).', 'Easy', 'MCQ', '', {'A': 'Content-Type', 'B': 'Accept', 'C': 'Allow', 'D': 'Expect'}, 'B', 'How does content negotiation use the Accept header?'),
    ('What is Content Negotiation in HTTP?', 'The mechanism where client and server negotiate the best media representation format (via `Accept`), language (`Accept-Language`), and compression encoding (`Accept-Encoding`).', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'What status code indicates server cannot provide requested representation (406 Not Acceptable)?'),
    ('What is the difference between a forward proxy and a reverse proxy?', 'A forward proxy represents the client and hides client identities from internet servers. A reverse proxy represents the servers and hides internal server topology from external clients.', 'Easy', 'Comparison', '', 'Which type of proxy is used for load balancing?'),
    ('What is a WebSocket Handshake?', 'An initial standard HTTP GET request with `Upgrade: websocket` and `Connection: Upgrade` headers. If the server supports WebSockets, it responds with `101 Switching Protocols`, upgrading the connection to full-duplex TCP.', 'Intermediate', 'Concept', 'GET /chat HTTP/1.1\nHost: server.example.com\nUpgrade: websocket\nConnection: Upgrade\nSec-WebSocket-Key: dGhlIHNhbXBsZSBub25jZQ==\n# Response:\nHTTP/1.1 101 Switching Protocols', 'What status code indicates successful protocol switching?'),
    ('What is the difference between `httpOnly` cookies and standard cookies?', '`httpOnly` cookies cannot be accessed by client-side JavaScript (`document.cookie`), protecting session identifiers from being stolen by XSS scripts.', 'Easy', 'Comparison', '', 'Can an `httpOnly` cookie still be sent with AJAX requests?'),
    ('What is the difference between authorization and authentication?', 'Authentication confirms WHO you are (verifying identity via username/password or token). Authorization determines WHAT you are allowed to do (permissions and access controls).', 'Easy', 'Comparison', '', 'Which occurs first in a web request?'),
    ('What is a JWT (JSON Web Token) and what are its three parts?', 'A compact, URL-safe means of representing claims between two parties. Three parts separated by dots: 1) Header (algorithm and token type). 2) Payload (claims, user ID, expiration). 3) Signature (HMAC or RSA hash of header + payload).', 'Easy', 'Concept', 'eyJhbGciOi... . eyJzdWIiOi... . SflKxwRJ...', 'Are JWT payloads encrypted or just Base64Url-encoded?'),
    ('Why is it dangerous to store sensitive secrets in a JWT payload without encryption?', 'JWT payloads are only Base64Url-encoded, NOT encrypted. Anyone who intercepts or possesses the token can decode and read all payload claims in plaintext.', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'What provides encryption for JWTs (JWE — JSON Web Encryption)?'),
    ('What is the purpose of the JWT signature?', 'The signature verifies that the token was issued by a trusted server and has not been tampered with or modified by the client or intermediaries.', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'How is the signature calculated?'),
    ('What is a Refresh Token and why is it used alongside short-lived Access Tokens?', 'An Access Token is short-lived (e.g. 15 minutes) for security. When it expires, the client uses a securely stored Refresh Token (e.g. 7 days, in HttpOnly cookie) to request a new Access Token without forcing the user to log in again.', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'What is refresh token rotation?'),
    ('What is Refresh Token Rotation?', 'Every time a refresh token is used to obtain a new access token, the server invalidates the old refresh token and issues a brand new one. If an invalidated refresh token is reused, all tokens in that family are immediately revoked (detecting token theft).', 'Intermediate', 'Concept', '', 'Why is refresh token rotation considered a security best practice?'),
    ('What is Cross-Site Scripting (XSS) via `innerHTML`?', "Assigning untrusted user-controlled strings to an element's `innerHTML` property can inject and execute `<img src=x onerror=alert(1)>` scripts. Safe alternative: `textContent` or DOM sanitization (DOMPurify).", 'Easy', 'Practical', '// VULNERABLE\nelement.innerHTML = userInput;\n// SAFE\nelement.textContent = userInput;', 'Why does `textContent` prevent XSS?'),
    ('Why does `element.textContent` completely prevent XSS?', 'Because `textContent` treats all input strictly as raw literal text string characters, never parsing HTML tags, entity references, or `<script>` elements.', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'What is DOMPurify?'),
    ('What is DOMPurify?', 'A widely used, trusted open-source DOM-only XSS sanitizer library for HTML, MathML, and SVG that strips dangerous tags and attributes from dirty HTML.', 'Easy', 'Concept', 'const clean = DOMPurify.sanitize(dirtyHtml);', 'When would you need DOMPurify in a React app?'),
    ('What is an Origin header in HTTP?', 'A header sent by the browser in cross-origin requests and POST/PUT requests indicating the scheme, domain, and port from which the request originated.', 'Easy', 'Concept', 'Origin: https://myapp.com', 'Can a web page modify its own `Origin` header in JavaScript (No, browser-restricted)?'),
    ('Can client-side JavaScript forge or alter the `Origin` or `Referer` headers in Fetch?', 'No. The browser strictly manages `Origin`, `Referer`, `Host`, and `Cookie` headers as forbidden header names that cannot be modified via JavaScript.', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'Why does the browser prohibit modifying forbidden headers?'),
    ('What is a Simple Request in CORS?', 'A cross-origin request that uses safe HTTP methods (GET, HEAD, POST) with standard CORS-safelisted headers and `Content-Type` of `application/x-www-form-urlencoded`, `multipart/form-data`, or `text/plain`, which executes without a preflight OPTIONS request.', 'Intermediate', 'Concept', '', 'Why does sending `Content-Type: application/json` trigger a CORS preflight request?'),
    ('What is `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` in CORS?', 'A server response header allowing the browser to expose the response to frontend JavaScript when the request included credentials (cookies, authorization headers, TLS client certificates).', 'Intermediate', 'Concept', 'Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true', 'Can `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` be used together with `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true`?'),
    ('Why is `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` prohibited when `Access-Control-Allow-Credentials: true` is set?', 'Browsers block this combination to prevent malicious sites from reading authenticated user data from public wildcard APIs. The server must specify the exact requesting origin.', 'Intermediate', 'Concept', '', 'What happens in browser console if both are returned?'),
    ('What is the difference between `null` origin and a standard origin in CORS?', 'A `null` origin is assigned to sandboxed iframes, local `file://` URLs, or requests generated from data URIs. Allowing `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: null` is considered a serious security risk.', 'Advanced', 'Concept', '', 'Why is allowing null origin dangerous?'),
    ('What is a Content Delivery Network (CDN) edge cache purge / invalidation?', 'An operation that clears cached assets across all global edge servers, forcing the CDN to fetch fresh versions from the origin server upon subsequent user requests.', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'Why is cache purging generally slower than cache busting with versioned hashes?'),
    ('What is Brotli compression and why is it superior to Gzip?', 'Brotli uses a modern dictionary-based LZ77 algorithm with a pre-defined static dictionary of over 13,000 common HTML/CSS/JS keywords, achieving significantly smaller compression sizes for web assets.', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'Can Brotli be used over unencrypted HTTP (No, browsers require HTTPS)?'),
    ('What is the difference between Minification and Compression?', 'Minification removes unnecessary characters from source code (whitespace, newlines, comments, shortening variable names) without altering functionality. Compression (gzip, brotli) reduces byte size using binary encoding algorithms.', 'Easy', 'Comparison', '', 'Should web assets be both minified AND compressed?'),
    ('What is Gzip compression?', 'A widely supported file compression format using the DEFLATE algorithm (combining LZ77 and Huffman coding) that compresses web text assets on average by 60-80%.', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'What response header signals Gzip compression (`Content-Encoding: gzip`)?'),
    ('What is HTTP/2 multiplexing advantage over HTTP/1.1 multiple connections?', 'HTTP/1.1 opened 6 separate TCP connections per domain, each requiring separate handshakes and slow-start phases. HTTP/2 sends all concurrent streams over a single TCP connection, reducing server memory and network congestion.', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'How many TCP connections does HTTP/2 typically maintain per domain (One)?'),
    ('What is the TCP Slow-Start mechanism?', 'An algorithm that balances data transmission rate by starting with a small number of packets (congestion window) and doubling the window size with each successful round of acknowledgments until packet loss occurs.', 'Intermediate', 'Concept', '', 'Why does TCP slow-start make reusing connections important?'),
    ('What is Keep-Alive in HTTP connections?', 'A mechanism that keeps the underlying TCP socket open after an HTTP transaction completes so subsequent requests can be sent over the same connection without performing another 3-way handshake.', 'Easy', 'Concept', 'Connection: keep-alive', 'How does Keep-Alive reduce latency?'),
    ('What is an HTTP Proxy Server?', 'An intermediary computer that acts as a gateway between a client seeking resources and the destination server providing those resources.', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', "What header does a proxy add to identify the client's original IP (`X-Forwarded-For`)?"),
    ('What is the `X-Forwarded-For` header?', 'A de facto standard header for identifying the originating IP address of a client connecting to a web server through an HTTP proxy or load balancer.', 'Easy', 'Concept', 'X-Forwarded-For: 203.0.113.195, 70.41.3.18, 150.172.238.178', 'Can `X-Forwarded-For` be spoofed by a client if not sanitized by a trusted proxy?'),
    ('What is the `X-Forwarded-Proto` header?', 'A header that identifies the protocol (HTTP or HTTPS) that a client used to connect to your reverse proxy, enabling backend servers to know if the client connection was secure.', 'Easy', 'Concept', 'X-Forwarded-Proto: https', 'Why is `X-Forwarded-Proto` needed when SSL termination occurs at the load balancer?'),
    ('What is SSL Termination at a Load Balancer?', 'The process where the reverse proxy / load balancer handles the incoming HTTPS TLS decryption and forwards decrypted plaintext HTTP requests to internal backend servers, offloading cryptographic CPU load.', 'Intermediate', 'Concept', '', 'What is the security trade-off of SSL termination?'),
    ('What is an API Gateway?', 'A server that acts as a single entry point for a microservices architecture, handling routing, authentication, rate limiting, logging, and protocol translation.', 'Intermediate', 'Concept', '', 'How is an API Gateway different from a simple reverse proxy?'),
    ('What is a Monolithic Architecture vs Microservices Architecture?', 'Monolith: all application code, database access, and features reside in a single deployable unit (simple to develop/test, harder to scale across teams). Microservices: application split into small, independently deployable services communicating over HTTP/gRPC.', 'Easy', 'Comparison', '', 'Which architecture is recommended for freshers starting a new project?'),
    ('Why is a Monolithic architecture usually recommended for fresher / early-stage projects?', 'Monoliths avoid distributed system complexities like network latency, distributed transactions, service discovery, cross-service debugging, and complex CI/CD deployments.', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'What is the main challenge of scaling a monolith?'),
    ('What is REST (Representational State Transfer)?', 'An architectural style for network applications based on stateless, client-server communication using standard HTTP methods (GET, POST, PUT, DELETE) to manipulate named resources identified by URIs.', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'Who defined REST in their 2000 doctoral dissertation (Roy Fielding)?'),
    ('What is an Idempotent HTTP method?', 'An HTTP method where making multiple identical requests has the exact same side-effect on the server state as making a single request (e.g. GET, PUT, DELETE, HEAD, OPTIONS).', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'Is POST idempotent (No)?'),
    ('Why is POST NOT an idempotent method?', 'Sending multiple identical POST requests typically creates multiple new resources (e.g. creating duplicate users or duplicate order records).', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'Why is PUT idempotent while POST is not?'),
    ('What is the difference between PUT and PATCH in REST APIs?', 'PUT replaces the entire resource representation with the provided payload. PATCH applies partial modifications to the resource, updating only the supplied fields.', 'Easy', 'Comparison', '', 'Is PATCH strictly idempotent according to RFC specifications?'),
    ('What is GraphQL and how does it differ from REST?', 'A query language for APIs where clients request exactly the fields they need in a single request, eliminating REST over-fetching and under-fetching issues, served through a single `/graphql` endpoint.', 'Easy', 'Comparison', '', 'What is over-fetching in REST?'),
    ('What is over-fetching in REST APIs?', 'When an endpoint returns more data fields than the client needs (e.g. `/api/users/1` returning 50 fields when the client only needed `name` and `avatar`).', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'What is under-fetching?'),
    ('What is under-fetching in REST APIs?', 'When a single endpoint does not provide enough data, forcing the client to make multiple successive round-trip requests (e.g. fetching user, then orders, then order items).', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'How does GraphQL solve under-fetching?'),
    ('What is a Webhook?', 'An automated HTTP POST callback sent by a server to a client-configured URL when a specific event occurs (e.g. Stripe notifying your server when a payment succeeds).', 'Easy', 'Concept', '', 'How do you verify the authenticity of an incoming webhook payload (HMAC signature verification)?'),
    ('How do you verify the authenticity of an incoming Webhook?', 'Verify the signature in the webhook header (e.g. `stripe-signature`) using the raw request body and your shared secret key via HMAC-SHA256.', 'Intermediate', 'Practical', "const expected = crypto.createHmac('sha256', secret).update(rawBody).digest('hex');\nconst isValid = crypto.timingSafeEqual(Buffer.from(signature), Buffer.from(expected));", 'Why is `crypto.timingSafeEqual` used instead of `===` for cryptographic signatures?'),
    ('What is a Timing Attack in web security and how does `timingSafeEqual` prevent it?', 'An attack where an attacker measures the microsecond response time of string comparison (`===` exits early on the first mismatched byte) to deduce correct secret values byte-by-byte. `timingSafeEqual` runs in constant time regardless of where mismatches occur.', 'Advanced', 'Concept', '', 'Where are constant-time comparisons mandatory in web applications?'),
]
